Summary: Rosetta 2: make it notice code written into another process
Category: cpu
Kind: workaround
Origin: crossover
  Version: 26.3.0
  CW-Hacks: 18947
  URL: https://media.codeweavers.com/pub/crossover/source/crossover-sources-26.3.0.tar.gz
  Files: dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
  Note: Sikarugir 10.0_8 ships it: its NtWriteVirtualMemory calls
    NtQueryVirtualMemory and NtProtectVirtualMemory twice after the server
    call (disassembly; no strings of its own, so the string inventory
    missed it)
  Changes: verbatim, apart from is_apple_silicon(), which
    ntdll-is-apple-silicon adds
Upstream: no
Why: NtWriteVirtualMemory writes another process's memory with
 mach_vm_write from wineserver, and Rosetta doesn't notice code changed
 that way: the target keeps running its old translation. After the write,
 if the target allocation's original protection (AllocationProtect) is
 executable, change the written range's protection to PAGE_NOACCESS and
 back from inside the target (NtProtectVirtualMemory on the process
 handle), so Rosetta retranslates. Armadillo, the DRM in Big Fish games,
 writes "jmp $" (EB FE) over its child's entry point, waits for the child
 to reach it, then writes the original bytes back; without this the child
 spins on the stale "jmp $" for ever and activation fails. Toggling the
 entry page's protection by hand inside a stuck gmActivator child (lldb,
 mprotect) let it continue as on Sikarugir 10.0_8 (2026-11-15).
Programs: Big Fish games (Armadillo DRM): activation fails, as gmActivator's
 child spins for ever on a stale "jmp $"
Seen on: macOS 26.7.2 (M1 Max): cross-process-code-write fails 3 of 3 with
 the toggle turned off (an interposer hiding sysctl.proc_translated)
Known issues: every page of an image has AllocationProtect
 PAGE_EXECUTE_WRITECOPY, so a write into an image's data (a trainer,
 launcher or DRM parent writing a running program's .data) also makes the
 page PAGE_NOACCESS for a moment, and another thread of the target that
 touches it faults: the child died after 4-5 writes, 12 of 12
 (cross-process-data-write; 10.0 build0 and the 11.22 quick build). Only
 the first page's protection is restored over a range that spans pages
 of different protection. The 11.22 copy fixes both; not yet ported.
Expects before it: ntdll-is-apple-silicon; is_apple_silicon(), split out of
 this patch's CW HACK 18947 (2026-11-26)
Status: 11.21: not yet in a build

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -6498,6 +6498,38 @@
     }
 
     return apple_silicon_status;
+}
+#endif
+
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+/* CW HACK 18947
+ * If mach_vm_write() is used to modify code cross-process (which is how we implement
+ * NtWriteVirtualMemory), Rosetta won't notice the change and will execute the "old" code.
+ *
+ * To work around this, after the write completes,
+ * toggle the executable bit (from inside the target process) on/off for any executable
+ * pages that were modified, to force Rosetta to re-translate it.
+ */
+static void toggle_executable_pages_for_rosetta( HANDLE process, void *addr, SIZE_T size )
+{
+    MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION info;
+    NTSTATUS status;
+    SIZE_T ret;
+
+    if (!is_apple_silicon())
+        return;
+
+    status = NtQueryVirtualMemory( process, addr, MemoryBasicInformation, &info, sizeof(info), &ret );
+
+    if (!status && (info.AllocationProtect & 0xf0))
+    {
+        DWORD origprot, noexec;
+        noexec = info.AllocationProtect & ~0xf0;
+        if (!noexec) noexec = PAGE_NOACCESS;
+
+        NtProtectVirtualMemory( process, &addr, &size, noexec, &origprot );
+        NtProtectVirtualMemory( process, &addr, &size, origprot, &noexec );
+    }
 }
 #endif
 
@@ -6521,6 +6553,10 @@
             size = reply->written;
         }
         SERVER_END_REQ;
+
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+        toggle_executable_pages_for_rosetta( process, addr, size );
+#endif
     }
     else
     {
